webnn: broadcast mismatched matmul batch dims before BATCH_MATMUL

WebNN's matmul validation allows the batch dims (every axis but the
trailing two) of the two operands to differ as long as they are
NumPy-broadcastable. GraphBuilderTflite::SerializeMatmul previously passed
such operands straight through to TFLite's BATCH_MATMUL unchanged. TFLite's
BATCH_MATMUL spec permits this, and the XNNPACK CPU kernel implements it
correctly, but not every BATCH_MATMUL backend does: some only handle the
case where one operand is unbatched (rank 2) and the other is batched, and
silently mis-compute cases where both operands are batched (rank >= 3, same
rank) but disagree on one or more batch axes -- see
33-litert-bmm-batch-broadcast.patch for the concrete GPU-delegate failure
this caused on the SAM encoder.

Fix this at the graph-builder level instead of per-backend: insert explicit
BROADCAST_TO ops ahead of BATCH_MATMUL for whichever operand has smaller
batch dims, so every backend always receives operands with matching batch
dims. This cannot change the result on backends that already broadcast
correctly (XNNPACK), it only moves which op performs the replication, and it
makes 33-litert-bmm-batch-broadcast.patch's GPU-side workaround dead code
(verified by reverting it and re-running the regression test below through
the real GPU delegate -- still passes).

Add WebNNGraphImplBackendTest.MatmulBatchDimsBroadcast, covering both an
outermost batch-axis broadcast and an inner batch-axis broadcast (the
harder case, since the outer axis already matches at a non-1 value).

Add matching WPT conformance test cases to matmul.https.any.js (float32 and
float16) for the inner-batch-axis-broadcast shape, which was not previously
covered by any existing broadcast test case there (all prior "(broadcast)"
cases only broadcast the outermost batch axis).

diff --git a/services/webnn/tflite/graph_builder_tflite.cc b/services/webnn/tflite/graph_builder_tflite.cc
index 268ed62b99..4fa767a333 100644
--- a/services/webnn/tflite/graph_builder_tflite.cc
+++ b/services/webnn/tflite/graph_builder_tflite.cc
@@ -8289,7 +8289,62 @@ auto GraphBuilderTflite::SerializeMatmul(const mojom::Matmul& matmul)
   ASSIGN_OR_RETURN(const TensorInfo output_tensor_info,
                    SerializeOutputTensorInfo(matmul.output_operand_id));
 
-  return SerializeMatmulOperation(a_tensor_info.index, b_tensor_info.index,
+  TensorIndex a_tensor_index = a_tensor_info.index;
+  TensorIndex b_tensor_index = b_tensor_info.index;
+
+  // WebNN's matmul allows the batch dims (every axis but the trailing two)
+  // of `a` and `b` to differ and broadcast against each other following
+  // NumPy rules. TFLite's BATCH_MATMUL spec allows this too and XNNPACK's
+  // CPU kernel implements it correctly, but not every BATCH_MATMUL backend
+  // does: some only handle the case where one operand is unbatched (rank
+  // 2) and the other is batched, and silently mis-compute the case where
+  // both operands are batched (rank >= 3, same rank) but disagree on one
+  // or more batch axes. Materialize the broadcast explicitly here with
+  // BROADCAST_TO so every backend always sees matching batch dims on both
+  // operands; this cannot change the result (XNNPACK already computes the
+  // same broadcast), it only moves which op performs the replication.
+  const std::vector<int32_t>& a_dims = a_tensor_info.dimensions;
+  const std::vector<int32_t>& b_dims = b_tensor_info.dimensions;
+  if (a_dims.size() >= 3 && a_dims.size() == b_dims.size() &&
+      !std::equal(a_dims.begin(), a_dims.end() - 2, b_dims.begin())) {
+    const size_t batch_rank = a_dims.size() - 2;
+    std::vector<int32_t> batch_dims(batch_rank);
+    for (size_t i = 0; i < batch_rank; ++i) {
+      batch_dims[i] = std::max(a_dims[i], b_dims[i]);
+    }
+    if (!std::ranges::equal(a_dims.begin(), a_dims.begin() + batch_rank,
+                            batch_dims.begin(), batch_dims.end())) {
+      std::vector<int32_t> a_broadcast_dims = batch_dims;
+      a_broadcast_dims.insert(a_broadcast_dims.end(), a_dims.end() - 2,
+                              a_dims.end());
+      ASSIGN_OR_RETURN(const TensorIndex broadcast_a_tensor_index,
+                       SerializeTemporaryTensorWithByteSizeCheck(
+                           a_broadcast_dims, a_tensor_info.data_type));
+      ASSIGN_OR_RETURN(
+          const OperatorOffset broadcast_a_op,
+          SerializeBroadcastToOperation(a_tensor_index, a_broadcast_dims,
+                                        broadcast_a_tensor_index));
+      operators_.emplace_back(broadcast_a_op);
+      a_tensor_index = broadcast_a_tensor_index;
+    }
+    if (!std::ranges::equal(b_dims.begin(), b_dims.begin() + batch_rank,
+                            batch_dims.begin(), batch_dims.end())) {
+      std::vector<int32_t> b_broadcast_dims = batch_dims;
+      b_broadcast_dims.insert(b_broadcast_dims.end(), b_dims.end() - 2,
+                              b_dims.end());
+      ASSIGN_OR_RETURN(const TensorIndex broadcast_b_tensor_index,
+                       SerializeTemporaryTensorWithByteSizeCheck(
+                           b_broadcast_dims, b_tensor_info.data_type));
+      ASSIGN_OR_RETURN(
+          const OperatorOffset broadcast_b_op,
+          SerializeBroadcastToOperation(b_tensor_index, b_broadcast_dims,
+                                        broadcast_b_tensor_index));
+      operators_.emplace_back(broadcast_b_op);
+      b_tensor_index = broadcast_b_tensor_index;
+    }
+  }
+
+  return SerializeMatmulOperation(a_tensor_index, b_tensor_index,
                                   output_tensor_info.index);
 }
 
